<LessonLayout title="Workbook &mdash; The Desire for God" cic="CCC 27-49" scripture="">

<section class="content">

<h2>Student Workbook</h2>
<p class="big-question">For use after class or in groups. Print this page or work in your notebook.</p>

<h3>1. Recalling the Essentials</h3>

<ol>
<li>What is the phrase from St. Augustine that opens the lesson? What does it mean in your own words?</li>
<li>Explain in your own words Fulton Sheen's image of the &laquo;God-shaped hole&raquo;. Why is it such a powerful image?</li>
<li>What does the Catechism mean when it states that man is &laquo;capable&raquo; of God (CCC 27)?</li>
<li>What are the two opposite errors about faith that the lesson points out? Explain each one briefly.</li>
<li>The Catechism mentions seven obstacles that can prevent man from recognizing God (CCC 29). Name at least five of them.</li>
<li>What role do witnesses and community play in the journey of faith, according to CCC 30? Why isn't it enough to search for God &laquo;on my own&raquo;?</li>
<li>What do numbers 31 through 35 of the Catechism say about the &laquo;ways of coming to know God&raquo;? Name at least three of those ways.</li>
<li>According to CCC 36-38, why does the Church teach that knowledge of God is not merely rational but also involves the heart and the will?</li>
</ol>

</section>

<section class="content">

<h3>2. For Reflection and Writing</h3>

<p>Answer each question sincerely. There are no &laquo;correct&raquo; answers to these personal questions.</p>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">1. What things do you seek to feel happy? (friends, success, rest, screens...)</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Make a list of 5 things you turn to when you feel empty or lonely.</p>
<p class="question-block__p" style="margin-top:8px">___________ ___________ ___________ ___________ ___________</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">2. Has any of those things given you happiness that lasts forever?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">If the answer is no, why do you think we keep looking in the same places? What does this tell us about the nature of human desire?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">3. Of the seven obstacles to recognizing God (CCC 29), which seems most common in your environment?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">(Rebellion against evil, ignorance, indifference, riches/cares, bad example, hostile currents, sin)</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Describe a concrete situation where you have seen that obstacle in action.</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">4. Have you ever felt that &laquo;something more&raquo; that you can't quite identify?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Describe the situation: When? Where? What were you doing? What did you feel?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">5. What person in your life has helped you seek truth or ask important questions?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">The Catechism says that &laquo;man needs the witness of others who teach him to seek God&raquo; (CCC 30). Who has been that witness for you? What did they do or say that left a mark on you?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">6. Think about the happiest moments of your life. What did they have in common?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">Do you think that in those moments you were closer to God, even if you weren't consciously thinking about it? Why?</p>
</div>

<div class="question-block">
<p class="question-block__q">7. Have you ever tried to &laquo;fill the hole&raquo; with something you knew wasn't right?</p>
<p class="question-block__p">What happened? How did you feel afterward? What did you learn from that experience?</p>
</div>

</section>

<section class="content" id="activities">

<h3>3. Practical Activities</h3>

<h4>A. The Map of the Heart</h4>
<p>Draw a large heart on a sheet of paper. Divide the heart into sections like puzzle pieces. In each section, write something that occupies space in your heart: your family, your friends, your dreams, your fears, your hobbies, school or work, social media... Leave one section empty, unfilled. That section is the &laquo;God-shaped hole&raquo;. Reflect and write: What are you putting there that isn't God? What would happen if you let God occupy that space? Why are you afraid to leave it empty?</p>

<h4>B. Psalm 42 in First Person</h4>
<p>Read Psalm 42 slowly, twice. Then write it in your own words, as if you were the one saying to God:</p>
<p style="border:1px dashed var(--border);border-radius:var(--radius-sm);padding:16px;margin:12px 0;color:var(--text-soft);font-style:italic">&laquo;As the deer longs for water, so my soul longs for you, my God...&raquo;</p>
<p><em>Continue your personal version of the psalm (6-8 lines). It's not about rhyming &mdash; it's about telling God, in your own words, what your soul feels:</em></p>
<p style="border-bottom:1px solid var(--border);margin-bottom:8px">&nbsp;</p>
